---
import PropertyPackPage from '@/components/property-pack/PropertyPackPage.astro';
import requiredProperties from '@/data/property-pack/required-properties.json';
import provenanceIndex from '../../../../source/03-standards/rml/provenance-index.json';
import {
  changedKindLexicalMatches,
  comparisonDimensions,
  continuityPoints,
  evidenceLinks,
  interpretationLimits,
  lexicalMatches,
  sameKindLexicalMatches,
  schemaDerivedCounts,
  propertyPackCounts,
} from '@/lib/model-comparison.mjs';

const kindLabel = (value) => value.replaceAll('-', ' ');
const provenancePaths = new Set(Object.values(provenanceIndex.leaves).map((entry) => entry.leaf_path));
const exactPathMatches = requiredProperties.filter((record) => provenancePaths.has(record.source.path)).length;
---
<PropertyPackPage
  title="PDTF schema lineage"
  description="The proven and unresolved lineage from the PDTF schema and its separate schema-derived ontology to the SPDTF Property Pack ontology candidate."
  category="Lineage and crosswalk"
  crumbs={[{ label: 'PDTF schema lineage' }]}
>
  <h1>PDTF schema lineage and semantic crosswalk</h1>

  <p class="lead v2-comparison-lead">
    The PDTF schema contains the source paths used to define the Property Pack. A separate
    draft ontology was derived from that schema, its dictionary, glossary and supporting
    evidence. The SPDTF Property Pack candidate reconsiders the bounded profile by
    contextual meaning; it is neither a renamed schema nor a revised list of terms.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--key" id="bottom-line">
    <p class="callout__label">Bottom line</p>
    <p>
      <strong>The main change is modelling authority and evidence discipline—not a move
      from JSON to RDF.</strong> A separate graph ontology was derived from the PDTF schema,
      but neither artefact has scheme authority. The Property Pack candidate asks
      domain-led questions afresh, keeps source facts separate from semantic proposals
      and assigns every candidate resource one explicit semantic home.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout callout--warn" id="crosswalk-status">
    <p class="callout__label">Crosswalk status</p>
    <p>
      <strong>All {requiredProperties.length} required Property Pack paths occur in the
      PDTF schema.</strong> Only <strong>{exactPathMatches}</strong> have an exact full-path
      entry in the schema-derived ontology provenance index. Consolidation and
      one-to-many modelling mean this is not a semantic coverage percentage. The
      maintained 451-row crosswalk from schema path to schema-derived construct or gap,
      and then to the Property Pack candidate, remains incomplete. It must record each
      retained, revised, consolidated, split or missing disposition.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="muted tiny">
    This report distinguishes the <strong>PDTF schema</strong>, the separate
    <strong>schema-derived ontology</strong>, and the <strong>Property Pack ontology
    candidate</strong>. The candidate is an accelerated SPDTF component, not an adoption,
    release, replacement or external-authority claim.
  </p>


  <h2 id="scale">Quantitative snapshot</h2>
  <div class="callout callout--warn">
    <p class="callout__label">Not a like-for-like size comparison</p>
    <p>
      The schema-derived ontology covers evidence from the broader PDTF schema and overlays.
      The candidate covers only the required Property Pack profile and consolidates repeated source paths into semantic patterns.
      These figures describe each corpus; they do not rank quality or completeness.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="v2-snapshot-grid">
    <section class="v2-snapshot v2-snapshot--current">
      <header>
        <span class="pill pill--info">Draft derived artefact</span>
        <h3>Ontology derived from the PDTF schema</h3>
        <p>A separate technical corpus organised through concern modules and industry views; it is not an endorsed scheme.</p>
      </header>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>{schemaDerivedCounts.resources}</dt><dd>OWL resources</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{schemaDerivedCounts.classes}</dt><dd>classes</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{schemaDerivedCounts.objectProperties}</dt><dd>object properties</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{schemaDerivedCounts.datatypeProperties}</dt><dd>datatype properties</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{schemaDerivedCounts.schemes}</dt><dd>value schemes · {schemaDerivedCounts.concepts} concepts</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{schemaDerivedCounts.shapes}</dt><dd>SHACL shapes · {schemaDerivedCounts.constraints} indexed constraints</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{schemaDerivedCounts.overlayProfiles}</dt><dd>overlay profiles</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{schemaDerivedCounts.exemplars}</dt><dd>diagnostic exemplars</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="v2-snapshot v2-snapshot--candidate">
      <header>
        <span class="pill pill--warn">Public review candidate</span>
        <h3>SPDTF · Property Pack ontology candidate</h3>
        <p>A separate candidate namespace with explicit context ownership and source-item traceability.</p>
      </header>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>{propertyPackCounts.resources}</dt><dd>OWL resources</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{propertyPackCounts.classes}</dt><dd>classes</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{propertyPackCounts.objectProperties}</dt><dd>object properties</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{propertyPackCounts.datatypeProperties}</dt><dd>datatype properties</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{propertyPackCounts.schemes}</dt><dd>value schemes · {propertyPackCounts.concepts} concepts</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{propertyPackCounts.shapes}</dt><dd>SHACL shapes · {propertyPackCounts.constraints} property constraints</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{propertyPackCounts.sourceItems}</dt><dd>required source data points</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{propertyPackCounts.topicConcepts}</dt><dd>source-topic concepts</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2 id="comparison">Side-by-side comparison</h2>
  <div class="v2-compare" role="table" aria-label="Schema-derived ontology and Property Pack ontology candidate comparison">
    <div class="v2-compare__head" role="row">
      <span role="columnheader">Dimension</span>
      <span role="columnheader">Schema-derived ontology · draft</span>
      <span role="columnheader">Property Pack ontology · review candidate</span>
    </div>
    {comparisonDimensions.map((dimension) => (
      <section class="v2-compare__row" id={dimension.id} role="row">
        <h3 class="v2-compare__dimension" role="rowheader">{dimension.label}</h3>
        <div class="v2-compare__cell" role="cell">
          <span class="v2-compare__cell-label">Schema-derived ontology · draft</span>
          <p>{dimension.schemaDerived}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="v2-compare__cell" role="cell">
          <span class="v2-compare__cell-label">Property Pack ontology · review candidate</span>
          <p>{dimension.propertyPack}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="v2-compare__implication" role="cell" aria-colspan="3">
          <strong>Why it matters</strong>
          <span>{dimension.implication}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="v2-compare__evidence" role="cell" aria-colspan="3">
          Evidence:
          {dimension.evidence.map((item, index) => (
            <><a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>{index < dimension.evidence.length - 1 ? ' · ' : ''}</>
          ))}
        </p>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="semantic-deltas">Concrete semantic differences</h2>

  <div class="v2-note-grid">
    <section>
      <h3>Both models are graphs</h3>
      <p>
        The schema-derived ontology moved beyond the JSON tree and made explicit identity,
        class and relationship decisions. The Property Pack candidate changes how those
        decisions are sourced and owned;
        it does not introduce graph modelling for the first time.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h3>The overlap is limited</h3>
      <p>
        Only <strong>{lexicalMatches.length}</strong> of {propertyPackCounts.resources} candidate resources share
        an exact local name with a schema-derived ontology resource. Only <strong>{sameKindLexicalMatches.length}</strong>
        also retain the same broad resource kind. Names alone do not establish equivalence.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="v2-name-comparison" aria-labelledby="same-kind-name-matches">
    <h3 id="same-kind-name-matches">Exact local-name matches with the same resource kind</h3>
    <p>
      This is a mechanical identifier check: each row uses the same local name in both
      models and is classified at the same broad level, such as a class or an object
      property. It identifies a useful starting point for crosswalk review; it does
      <strong>not</strong> establish that the definitions, identity rules, relationships or
      constraints mean the same thing.
    </p>
    <p class="v2-name-comparison__summary">
      <strong>{sameKindLexicalMatches.length} names to review.</strong> Compare the meaning and
      evidence behind each pair before treating it as continuity.
    </p>
    <div class="v2-name-matrix" role="table" aria-label="Exact local-name matches with the same resource kind">
      <div class="v2-name-matrix__head" role="row">
        <span role="columnheader">Local name</span>
        <span role="columnheader">Schema-derived ontology</span>
        <span role="columnheader">Property Pack candidate</span>
      </div>
      {sameKindLexicalMatches.map((item) => (
        <div class="v2-name-matrix__row" role="row">
          <p class="v2-name-matrix__name" role="cell" data-label="Local name">
            <code>{item.localName}</code><span class="sr-only">{` ${kindLabel(item.schemaDerivedKind)}`}</span>
          </p>
          <span role="cell" data-label="Schema-derived ontology">{kindLabel(item.schemaDerivedKind)}</span>
          <span role="cell" data-label="Property Pack candidate">{kindLabel(item.propertyPackKind)}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="v2-name-comparison" aria-labelledby="reclassified-name-matches">
    <h3 id="reclassified-name-matches">Exact local-name matches that were reclassified</h3>
    <p>
      These names also occur in both models, but their broad resource kind has changed.
      For example, a term may be a class in one model and a property in the other. This
      is a clear signal not to infer a mapping from spelling alone: the modelling role
      has already changed and needs an explicit decision under the
      <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">Category 8 mapping method</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="v2-name-comparison__summary">
      <strong>{changedKindLexicalMatches.length} reclassification questions.</strong> Each row
      needs a decision on whether the two uses are related, distinct or simply coincidental.
    </p>
    <div class="v2-name-matrix" role="table" aria-label="Exact local-name matches with different resource kinds">
      <div class="v2-name-matrix__head" role="row">
        <span role="columnheader">Local name</span>
        <span role="columnheader">Schema-derived ontology</span>
        <span role="columnheader">Property Pack candidate</span>
      </div>
      {changedKindLexicalMatches.map((item) => (
        <div class="v2-name-matrix__row" role="row">
          <p class="v2-name-matrix__name" role="cell" data-label="Local name">
            <code>{item.localName}</code><span class="sr-only">{` ${kindLabel(item.schemaDerivedKind)}`}</span>
          </p>
          <span role="cell" data-label="Schema-derived ontology">{kindLabel(item.schemaDerivedKind)}</span>
          <span role="cell" data-label="Property Pack candidate">{kindLabel(item.propertyPackKind)}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="callout callout--warn">
    <p class="callout__label">A substantive identity question remains open</p>
    <p>
      The schema-derived ontology defines <a href="/pdtf/Property">Property</a> through spatial-material continuity
      and explicitly treats UPRN as a contingent identifier, not the identity criterion.
      The Property Pack ontology candidate <a href="/development/property-pack/resources/common/Property">Property candidate</a> currently associates
      continuity with the same assigned UPRN. That is a semantic difference for working-group
      review—not a settled SPDTF decision.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="v2-note-grid">
    <section>
      <h3>Present in the schema-derived ontology, outside the initial Property Pack scope</h3>
      <p>
        Examples include Transaction, Person, Organisation, Buyer, Seller, Claim,
        Evidence and Trust Framework. Their absence from this candidate does not retire them; the
        451-item Property Pack ontology candidate simply does not yet justify every broader schema-derived concept.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h3>Prominent Property Pack ontology candidate patterns</h3>
      <p>
        Boundary Statement, Disclosure Statement, Fixture Schedule, Register Extract,
        Search Finding, Utility Service Record and Data Sharing Authorisation make
        Property Pack meaning more explicit within proposed semantic homes.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2 id="continuity">What carries forward from PDTF schema</h2>
  <ul class="v2-check-list">
    {continuityPoints.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2 id="implications">What this means in practice</h2>
  <div class="v2-card-grid">
    <section class="v2-card">
      <span class="pill pill--info">Working groups</span>
      <h3>Review meaning, not ontology syntax</h3>
      <p>Test definitions, identity criteria, context ownership, relationships and constraints against real domain evidence.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="v2-card">
      <span class="pill pill--info">Current implementers</span>
      <h3>Keep using the current contracts</h3>
      <p>The Property Pack ontology candidate is not a replacement schema or migration target. Current integrations remain anchored to the published PDTF schema implementation.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="v2-card">
      <span class="pill pill--info">Interoperability group</span>
      <h3>Challenge the Common boundary</h3>
      <p>Review genuinely shared meaning and propose mappings without overriding each bounded context's internal vocabulary. Use the <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">governed mapping workflow</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="v2-card">
      <span class="pill pill--info">Model team</span>
      <h3>Make migration explicit</h3>
      <p>Record reviewed equivalence, transformation and projection decisions before generating replacement schemas or forms.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2 id="limits">What the comparison does not prove</h2>
  <ul class="v2-limit-list">
    {interpretationLimits.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2 id="evidence">Evidence and method</h2>
  <p>
    The figures are calculated at build time from the current generated ontology model,
    the committed schema-derived ontology profile and exemplar directories, and the isolated Property Pack ontology candidate
    projections. The lexical comparison uses exact local names only and deliberately
    makes no equivalence claim. Narrative conclusions are constrained by the recorded
    scope and architecture decisions.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {evidenceLinks.map((item) => <li><a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a></li>)}
  </ul>
</PropertyPackPage>
